# Theming Isolation Plan - make rebranding a one-file edit

Status: DRAFT (awaiting decision)
Last updated: 2026-07-24

This plan evaluates how to make the hub's styling easy for a fork to rebrand
without editing scattered CSS. It is a planning document - nothing is
implemented yet.

---

## 1. The problem

The hub's brand (the warm cream + gold "Astro Content Hub" look) is baked into
`src/styles/global.css` alongside all the structural/component CSS. A fork that
wants its own identity currently has to:

- Hunt through a 1500-line `global.css` for every hard-coded brand value.
- Know which `--color-*` tokens are "brand" (rebrand these) vs "structural"
  (leave these). The distinction is not marked.
- Catch the **scattered hard-coded** brand values that bypass the tokens
  entirely (see audit below).

There is no single, obvious, documented entry point for "rebrand this site."
Every org using the template will want different colors/fonts - the current
shape makes that a spelunking exercise.

## 2. Goal

- **One file to rebrand.** A fork changes its colors + fonts by editing a
  single, well-commented file (and its dark-mode counterpart block).
- **No behavior change.** Dark mode, the toggle, and every component keep
  working; only the *values* move.
- **Discoverable.** A `THEMING.md` and a README section point forks straight at
  that file.
- **No new runtime system.** CSS custom properties are the web-standard,
  zero-JS theming mechanism - the plan builds on them, not on config-driven
  CSS generation.

## 3. Approach considered

### Option C - Isolate brand tokens + document them  [RECOMMENDED]

- Split the design tokens into **brand** (colors, accent, fonts) vs
  **structural** (spacing, radius, shadows, transition).
- Move the brand tokens (light + dark) into a dedicated
  `src/styles/theme.css`, clearly commented as "edit this to rebrand."
- `global.css` `@import`s `theme.css` first, then defines structural tokens +
  reset + components. Components keep using `var(--color-*)` exactly as today.
- Replace the scattered hard-coded brand values with tokens (see audit).
- Add `THEMING.md` + a README section.

**Why not config-driven (Option D):** moving tokens to `src/config/theme.ts`
and generating `:root` from it adds build-time machinery for no real benefit on
a static site - CSS custom properties already *are* the config surface, and
every frontend dev knows them. The problem is discoverability and isolation,
not power. Option C fixes the actual problem with zero new code.

**Why not named runtime themes (Option E):** multiple switchable brand themes
at runtime is overkill for a template and couples brand to the theme-toggle
mechanism. Out of scope.

## 4. Token audit (current state)

`global.css` `:root` mixes brand and structural tokens in one block. The
brand-affecting tokens are:

- Colors: `--color-bg`, `--color-bg-alt`, `--color-surface`, `--color-border`,
  `--color-border-light`.
- Text: `--color-text`, `--color-text-secondary`, `--color-text-muted`.
- Accent: `--color-accent`, `--color-accent-hover`, `--color-accent-subtle`.
- Fonts: `--font-sans`, `--font-mono`.

(`--color-bg-dark` is the fixed dark surface for code blocks/buttons/CTA -
intentionally dark in both themes; it stays a brand token but is documented as
"rarely changed.")

Structural tokens (stay in `global.css`): `--container-max`, `--section-gap`,
`--section-gap-sm`, `--radius-*`, `--shadow-*`, `--transition`.

### Scattered hard-coded brand values (bypass tokens)

These must be tokenized so a rebrand catches them:

| Where | Value | New token |
|-------|-------|-----------|
| CTA gradient (line ~428) | `linear-gradient(..., var(--color-accent) 0%, #b8860b 50%, #8b6914 100%)` | `--color-accent` (start) + new `--color-accent-2` / `--color-accent-3` stops, or a `--gradient-accent` token |
| Text on dark surfaces (code, buttons, CTA, terminal) | `#f0ede7`, `#fff` (many sites) | `--color-on-dark` (light text on intentional dark surfaces) |
| Terminal bg / macOS dots | `#1c1c1a`, `#ff5f56`/`#ffbd2e`/`#27c93f` | Leave as decorative constants (traffic-light dots are theme-independent), but move the terminal bg to `--color-bg-dark` or a `--color-terminal-bg` token |

Most of the `#fff` usages are "text on a dark/accent button" - those become
`var(--color-on-dark)`. This also fixes a latent dark-mode bug (white text on
white-ish would be invisible if a fork set a light accent) by making the
contrast explicit.

## 5. Scope

### New file: `src/styles/theme.css`

```css
/* ============================================================
   astro-content-hub - BRAND TOKENS
   Edit this file (and the [data-theme='dark'] block) to rebrand
   the site. See THEMING.md. Everything else is structural and
   lives in global.css.
   ============================================================ */
:root {
  /* Page surfaces */
  --color-bg: #faf9f7;
  --color-bg-alt: #f2f0eb;
  --color-surface: #ffffff;
  --color-border: #e6e4df;
  --color-border-light: #f0ede7;

  /* Intentionally-dark surfaces (code blocks, buttons, CTA) - stay dark in
     both themes. Rarely changed. */
  --color-bg-dark: #141413;
  --color-on-dark: #f0ede7;          /* text on the dark surfaces above */

  /* Text */
  --color-text: #141413;
  --color-text-secondary: #6b6860;
  --color-text-muted: #99968e;

  /* Accent (brand color) */
  --color-accent: #d4a853;
  --color-accent-hover: #c09940;
  --color-accent-2: #b8860b;         /* gradient midpoint */
  --color-accent-3: #8b6914;         /* gradient endpoint */
  --color-accent-subtle: rgba(212, 168, 83, 0.12);

  /* Fonts */
  --font-sans: 'Inter', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', sans-serif;
  --font-mono: 'JetBrains Mono', 'SF Mono', 'Fira Code', monospace;
}

:root[data-theme='dark'] {
  /* Override ONLY the brand tokens above for dark mode. Structural tokens
     (spacing/radius/shadows) in global.css stay the same. */
  --color-bg: #141413;
  --color-bg-alt: #1c1b19;
  --color-surface: #1e1d1b;
  --color-border: #2e2c29;
  --color-border-light: #262421;
  --color-text: #f0ede7;
  --color-text-secondary: #a8a59d;
  --color-text-muted: #7a766e;
  --color-accent: #e0b85e;
  --color-accent-hover: #ecc676;
  --color-accent-subtle: rgba(224, 184, 94, 0.14);
}
```

### `src/styles/global.css`

- Add `@import './theme.css';` as the **first** line (CSS `@import` must precede
  other rules). Remove the brand tokens + the dark `:root[data-theme='dark']`
  block from `global.css` (they now live in `theme.css`).
- Keep `color-scheme` declarations (light in `:root`, dark in the dark block)
  in `theme.css` next to the tokens they describe.
- Replace scattered hard-coded values with the new tokens:
  - `#fff` / `#f0ede7` on dark surfaces -> `var(--color-on-dark)`.
  - The CTA gradient stops -> `var(--color-accent-2)` / `var(--color-accent-3)`.

### `THEMING.md` (new, repo root)

A short, fork-oriented guide: "to rebrand, edit `src/styles/theme.css`" -
covers colors, fonts, accent, the dark-mode block, and the gradient stops.
Points out `--color-on-dark` for text-on-dark-surface contrast. Notes that
structural tokens (spacing/radius/shadows) live in `global.css` and rarely
need changing.

### `README.md`

- Add a "Customize the look" bullet pointing at `theme.css` + `THEMING.md`
  (replacing/augmenting the existing note that points at `global.css`).

### No code / component changes

Components already use `var(--color-*)` - they are unaffected. The
`ThemeToggle.astro` and the no-FOUC script are unaffected (they flip
`data-theme`, which `theme.css`'s dark block responds to). Dark mode keeps
working for free because the dark block overrides the same token names.

## 6. Migration steps

1. Create `src/styles/theme.css` with the brand tokens (light + dark), copied
   from the current `global.css` `:root` block.
2. Add the new tokens (`--color-on-dark`, `--color-accent-2/3`) to both blocks.
3. In `global.css`: prepend `@import './theme.css';`, delete the brand tokens +
   dark block from `:root`.
4. Sweep `global.css` for hard-coded `#fff`/`#f0ede7` on dark surfaces and the
   CTA gradient stops, replacing with the new tokens.
5. Build; verify light + dark render identically to before (this is a pure
   move + tokenize, so output should be pixel-identical).
6. Write `THEMING.md` + README section.

## 7. Risks & mitigations

- **CSS `@import` order.** `@import` must be the first statement in the file.
   Vite/Astro handle it; verify the compiled CSS has theme tokens first.
- **Missed hard-coded value.** A grep sweep (the audit in section 4) catches
   them; the build + a visual diff of `dist/` confirms parity.
- **Dark-mode regression.** Pure move means the dark block must override the
   exact same token names. A before/after `dist/` diff on a dark page confirms.
- **`color-scheme` placement.** Keep it next to its theme block in `theme.css`
   so native scrollbars/form controls still adapt.

## 8. Verification

- `npm run build` -> 0 errors/warnings.
- Light and dark themes render identically to pre-refactor (diff the compiled
  CSS: same token values, just sourced from two files).
- Every `#fff`/`#f0ede7` on a dark surface now uses `var(--color-on-dark)`.
- `THEMING.md` exists and README links it; editing `theme.css` alone changes
  the brand (sanity-check by flipping `--color-accent`).

## 9. Effort

Small-medium. Mostly a move + a grep sweep + a doc. No logic changes, no new
deps, no routes. Lower risk than the i18n plan. ~0.5-1 session.

## 10. Decision needed

- **Option C vs D vs E?** (recommend C)
- **Accept new tokens** `--color-on-dark`, `--color-accent-2/3`? (recommend
  yes - they close the hard-coded gaps and a latent dark-mode contrast bug)
- **`theme.css` as a separate file vs a clearly-marked block at the top of
  `global.css`?** (recommend separate file - it is the literal "one file to
  rebrand" promise; a block inside a 1500-line file is less discoverable)
